content.thinking:
The previous audit flagged an issue that the `controller_has_matching_land` function only checks runtime object subtypes (`o.subtypes`), not registry data (`registry.card_data()`). However, I need to independently verify this finding.

Looking at the code:

```rust
fn controller_has_matching_land(state: &GameState, object_id: ObjectId) -> bool {
    let controller = match state.get_object(object_id) {
        Some(o) => o.controller,
        None => return false,
    };
    state.objects_in_zone(Zone::Battlefield, controller)
        .iter()
        .any(|o| {
            o.id != object_id
                && (o.subtypes.iter().any(|s| s == "Mountain")
                    || o.subtypes.iter().any(|s| s == "Plains"))
        })
}
```

This only checks `o.subtypes`, not the registry data. For real cards (non-tokens), subtypes are stored in the card registry's `CardData`, not on the `GameObject.subtypes` field. The `GameObject.subtypes` field comment says "Subtypes on this object (for tokens — regular cards use CardData.subtypes via registry)." So for a non-token card, this function would miss the land subtypes entirely since it's only looking at the runtime field instead of querying the registry.

Looking at how `check_condition` handles this in `state.rs`, it checks both the object's subtypes AND the registry data, falling back gracefully if the card isn't found. The `controller_has_matching_land` function needs the same approach to work correctly with real cards.

Now I need to trace through how basic lands like Mountain and Plains actually get their subtypes set up when they're created. Let me check the `create_object` function...

Examining object creation...

So objects start with an empty `subtypes` vector by default, which means the subtypes must be coming from the registry instead. I should look at how the engine handles this when lands enter the battlefield to see if subtypes are being copied from the card data to the object.

